     \nn      Matches a newline embedded in the pattern space (as
             left there by NN).

     \cc      Matches the literal character _c even if it would
             otherwise be special.

     If -ss is in effect, every letter typed in a pattern (whether
     alone, in a character class, or as the range endpoints of a
     character class) matches either its upper- or lower-case
     form in the pattern space.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     -ee   The next argument is one or more sseedd commands,
          separated by newlines.  More than one -ee (and -ff) argu-
          ment may be given; the commands are run in the order
          given.

     -ff   The next argument names a file of sseedd commands, one
          command per line.

     -nn   Suppress the automatic writing of the pattern space at
          the end of each cycle; only explicit pp, PP, ll, and
          similar output commands produce output.

     -ss   Fold case: a letter in a pattern matches either its up-
          per- or lower-case form in the input.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     sseedd reports each error found while compiling the commands,
     with the number of the command line it occurred on, and ex-
     its without processing any input if there were any.
     Execution-time errors (such as a buffer overflow) are also
     reported, with a diagnostic and no line number.

NNOOTTEESS
     The line, pattern, and hold buffers are each limited to 512
     characters; a label name or a file name given to ww or rr is
     truncated to 15 characters.  Up to nine \( ...  \) groups
     are allowed per pattern.
